WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.520
We're now going to take a look at some of the optimization

2
00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.839
view modes Unreal Engine 5 provides

3
00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:09.560
to help us visually debug performance issues

4
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:13.560
or better understand areas that could be performance

5
00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.579
issues inside of our levels.

6
00:00:16.579 --> 00:00:18.160
The first one we're going to talk about

7
00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:19.120
is lighting complexity.

8
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:20.359
But before we do that, I want

9
00:00:20.359 --> 00:00:22.039
to pop over to the editor really quick

10
00:00:22.039 --> 00:00:25.120
and show you where a lot of these view modes live.

11
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.879
And that is underneath the view mode settings here,

12
00:00:27.879 --> 00:00:29.320
optimization view modes.

13
00:00:29.339 --> 00:00:31.660
And we have our lighting complexity, light map density,

14
00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.899
stationary light overlap, shader complexity, shader complexity

15
00:00:34.899 --> 00:00:40.219
in quads, quad overdraw, material, LWC, function usage,

16
00:00:40.219 --> 00:00:42.299
and some others for texture streaming.

17
00:00:42.299 --> 00:00:46.380
So what does this lighting complexity do?

18
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.920
Well, lighting complexity is a visualization mode

19
00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.740
that helps you show how many non-static lights, which

20
00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:55.380
are dynamic or movable lights, are affecting your geometry.

21
00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.619
It's a really quick way to understand where you're seeing

22
00:00:57.619 --> 00:01:00.880
lighting might be heavy and, therefore, more performant

23
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.179
expensive.

24
00:01:02.179 --> 00:01:06.870
To interpret this, Unreal uses a scale that starts at black

25
00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:08.709
and goes to white.

26
00:01:08.709 --> 00:01:12.840
And as the scale starts to approach white,

27
00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.469
the values that it's showing us

28
00:01:15.469 --> 00:01:20.069
indicate the light is growing more and more expensive.

29
00:01:20.069 --> 00:01:23.709
We can use this mode to help visualize areas of our levels

30
00:01:23.709 --> 00:01:25.629
that are being hit by multiple lights

31
00:01:25.629 --> 00:01:29.590
so we can make better decisions about how to adjust lighting

32
00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.209
in that area.

33
00:01:31.209 --> 00:01:33.890
Oftentimes, it can be difficult to understand

34
00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:37.730
how your lights are interacting with one another.

35
00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:41.609
And this tool helps you make those decisions.

36
00:01:41.609 --> 00:01:44.489
Let's take a look at how that works in the editor.

37
00:01:44.489 --> 00:01:46.870
So again, we're going to come up here to our lit.

38
00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:49.730
We're going to go up to our optimization view modes.

39
00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:52.209
And we're going to go to our stationary light overlap.

40
00:01:52.209 --> 00:01:54.530
I'm just going to hold down L and click.

41
00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:56.469
Not stationary, light overlap.

42
00:01:56.469 --> 00:01:56.969
I'm sorry.

43
00:01:56.969 --> 00:02:00.810
We're going to come over here and we want our light complexity.

44
00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:02.069
There we go.

45
00:02:02.069 --> 00:02:05.930
And then I'm going to just hold down Alt and move and move.

46
00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:08.270
Keep holding down Alt. That's going to make some copies.

47
00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:12.590
So what I meant earlier when I said more informed choices

48
00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.789
is if we come here to our light attenuation radius

49
00:02:14.789 --> 00:02:17.590
and say we set this to something like 100,

50
00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:19.770
you can see here now we have our light.

51
00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.069
It's still kind of expensive.

52
00:02:21.069 --> 00:02:23.210
But as we move other lights around,

53
00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:25.710
or maybe we set this to like 500, you can see here

54
00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:28.150
now we can make a little bit more informed choice

55
00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:30.870
about how these lights are going to interact

56
00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:32.110
with one another.

57
00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:33.830
Maybe we have something like this.

58
00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:34.669
Oops, not like that.

59
00:02:34.669 --> 00:02:37.430
I wanted to grab that axis and just move it over like this.

60
00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:41.210
So we have something like that that's giving us

61
00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:42.590
the lights where we need them.

62
00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:46.009
But we have a better idea of how everything

63
00:02:46.009 --> 00:02:48.449
is interacting with one another.

64
00:02:48.449 --> 00:02:50.349
So remember some things to take away from this.

65
00:02:50.349 --> 00:02:52.590
Keeping your lighting complexity and the dark blue

66
00:02:52.849 --> 00:02:55.949
to bright green range for optimal performance.

67
00:02:55.949 --> 00:02:59.389
And red areas are warnings that it's time to simplify

68
00:02:59.389 --> 00:03:03.379
or optimizing your lighting setup in that particular area.

69
00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:06.199
The next optimization of view mode I want to talk about

70
00:03:06.199 --> 00:03:07.800
is quad overdraw.

71
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:09.840
So quad overdraw is a visualization mode

72
00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.419
that helps you understand how efficiently your geometry

73
00:03:12.419 --> 00:03:14.879
is being rendered on the GPU.

74
00:03:14.879 --> 00:03:18.280
GPU processes pixels in a two by two quad,

75
00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:19.939
not individual pixels.

76
00:03:19.939 --> 00:03:21.800
This means that when you have very small

77
00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:25.199
or thin triangles, smaller than a two by two pixel area,

78
00:03:25.199 --> 00:03:29.199
the GPU has to process the full quad for each one,

79
00:03:29.199 --> 00:03:31.240
which results in a lot of extra work,

80
00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:32.759
even though most of those pixels

81
00:03:32.759 --> 00:03:36.650
might not actually contribute to the final image.

82
00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:40.210
This matters because high quad overdraw means your GPU

83
00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:44.210
is wasting cycles, shading tiny triangles,

84
00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.889
which can seriously impact performance,

85
00:03:46.889 --> 00:03:50.990
especially when we have very dense, detailed scenes.

86
00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:54.030
This is also super common in high poly models,

87
00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:57.030
foliage and nanite fallback messes

88
00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:59.590
that are not properly optimized.

89
00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:03.129
The quad overdraw view mode shows which messes

90
00:04:03.129 --> 00:04:05.930
are suffering from excessive overdraw.

91
00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:08.469
Blue indicates low overdraw,

92
00:04:08.469 --> 00:04:10.669
which is good and what you want to see.

93
00:04:10.669 --> 00:04:13.629
Green to red indicates high overdraw,

94
00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:16.930
meaning the GPU is doing a lot of unnecessary work

95
00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:18.959
in those areas.

96
00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:22.720
You can reduce quad overdraw by using LODs

97
00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:26.019
to remove or simplify small thin triangles

98
00:04:26.019 --> 00:04:27.759
that can't be seen at distance

99
00:04:27.759 --> 00:04:31.600
or optimizing meshes to avoid excessive detail

100
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:34.870
in areas that don't contribute visually.

101
00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:37.730
Ensuring that meshes also have proper distance

102
00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:43.389
for the view that they are from the camera can also help.

103
00:04:43.389 --> 00:04:45.189
The key thing that you should take away from this

104
00:04:45.189 --> 00:04:49.750
is if you want quad overdraw to be as blue as possible,

105
00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:52.730
this means that your geometry is efficient

106
00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:56.079
and your GPU will render your scene

107
00:04:56.079 --> 00:04:58.920
much faster and smoother.

108
00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.339
The nanite visualization mode that is available

109
00:05:02.339 --> 00:05:07.339
shows you how nanite is being constructed for your scene.

110
00:05:07.899 --> 00:05:09.819
Let's take a look at these individually,

111
00:05:09.819 --> 00:05:12.540
starting right up here with the triangles.

112
00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:15.319
So these triangles displays all of the triangles

113
00:05:15.319 --> 00:05:18.920
that the nanite meshes in your current scene.

114
00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:20.899
This is useful for seeing mesh density

115
00:05:20.899 --> 00:05:24.540
and verifying how nanite is handling high poly geometry.

116
00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:26.060
If you zoom in, you can actually see

117
00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:28.660
how many triangles nanite is processing

118
00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:30.000
often in the millions.

119
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:30.920
And this is really cool

120
00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:32.319
because it's still doing all of that

121
00:05:32.319 --> 00:05:35.120
while maintaining real-time performance.

122
00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.339
Now the clusters view shows colored groupings of triangle

123
00:05:38.339 --> 00:05:40.100
that nanite uses internally.

124
00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.300
Each color represents a cluster

125
00:05:42.939 --> 00:05:44.139
or a logical group of triangles

126
00:05:44.139 --> 00:05:46.699
that nanite renders as a single unit.

127
00:05:46.699 --> 00:05:48.220
This view helps you understand

128
00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.480
how nanite's cluster-based rendering system

129
00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:52.579
is organizing and batching geometry

130
00:05:52.579 --> 00:05:56.259
for performance efficiency.

131
00:05:56.259 --> 00:06:00.189
Instances visualize a unique color

132
00:06:00.189 --> 00:06:01.709
to each instance in the scene.

133
00:06:01.709 --> 00:06:03.389
This is useful for identifying

134
00:06:03.389 --> 00:06:05.629
and verifying instanced static meshes,

135
00:06:05.629 --> 00:06:07.389
especially when working with

136
00:06:07.389 --> 00:06:09.269
instanced static mesh components

137
00:06:09.269 --> 00:06:12.910
or hierarchical instanced static mesh components.

138
00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:15.829
The primitive view colors all components

139
00:06:15.829 --> 00:06:18.029
of an instant mesh with the same color.

140
00:06:18.029 --> 00:06:19.629
Unlike instances view,

141
00:06:19.629 --> 00:06:22.470
this differentiates individual instances.

142
00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:24.350
This mode will help you see all components

143
00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.350
that share the same base mesh

144
00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:29.500
as one unified color group.

145
00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.439
Overdraw, which is very important,

146
00:06:31.439 --> 00:06:33.980
visualizes how much overdraw is happening

147
00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:35.500
in your scene geometry.

148
00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:36.779
You'll notice that a smaller,

149
00:06:36.779 --> 00:06:39.100
closely stacked objects like foliage

150
00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:42.100
or detailed props will create more overdraw

151
00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:44.660
than larger single surface meshes.

152
00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:46.420
This mode helps identify areas

153
00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:50.269
where rendering efficiency can be improved.

154
00:06:50.269 --> 00:06:53.829
Material ID view shows distinct colors

155
00:06:53.829 --> 00:06:56.589
for each material ID applied to nanite meshes.

156
00:06:56.589 --> 00:06:58.910
This is great for checking material assignments,

157
00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:00.290
multi-material setups,

158
00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:03.389
or ensuring that materials are applied consistently

159
00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:06.300
across complex assets.

160
00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:09.379
Raster bins shows you programmable GPU features

161
00:07:09.379 --> 00:07:12.819
such as tessellation and other rasterization tasks.

162
00:07:12.819 --> 00:07:15.180
This is primarily used for debugging

163
00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:16.420
advanced rendering paths

164
00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:19.180
and verifying how nanite is dividing geometry

165
00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:22.199
into programmable raster events.

166
00:07:22.199 --> 00:07:24.779
The evaluate world position offset view

167
00:07:24.779 --> 00:07:27.220
shows which meshes or materials

168
00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:28.860
are using world position offset.

169
00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:32.339
This is important because WPO can affect

170
00:07:32.339 --> 00:07:34.620
nanite performance and stability.

171
00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:37.939
Nanite doesn't support all types of vertex deformation

172
00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:40.939
and using this view can help you quickly identify assets

173
00:07:40.939 --> 00:07:43.899
relying on WPO or world position offset

174
00:07:43.899 --> 00:07:47.250
so you can quickly adjust them if needed.

175
00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:49.810
Remember that this view mode here

176
00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:52.870
can only be activated if you are utilizing

177
00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:54.689
a nanite mesh inside of your scene.

178
00:07:54.689 --> 00:07:56.329
So if I come back over here,

179
00:07:56.329 --> 00:07:58.129
I'm gonna come to my lighting complexity,

180
00:07:58.129 --> 00:07:59.250
just go back to lit,

181
00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:02.250
and if we come here to our nanite and we go to overview,

182
00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:04.889
I don't have any type of nanite mesh in here,

183
00:08:04.889 --> 00:08:07.930
but if I came into and added a,

184
00:08:08.889 --> 00:08:10.170
let's see here, we'll just right click

185
00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:11.790
and we'll do nanite.

186
00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:14.089
Nanite is enabled on this and I pull this in,

187
00:08:14.089 --> 00:08:16.730
then we're gonna get this view populated

188
00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:20.649
with a nanite mesh right there.

189
00:08:20.649 --> 00:08:23.589
So remember that if you're not seeing anything,

190
00:08:23.589 --> 00:08:25.490
it's probably because you do not have

191
00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:28.399
a nanite mesh placed inside of your scene.

192
00:08:28.399 --> 00:08:32.740
Now, one of the things that I want to call out

193
00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:36.559
specifically with nanite is nanites overdraw.

194
00:08:36.559 --> 00:08:38.340
Now, once you've determined that nanite

195
00:08:38.919 --> 00:08:40.100
may be causing performance issues,

196
00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:43.100
the overdraw visualization tool can help you pinpoint

197
00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.059
where those problems are.

198
00:08:45.059 --> 00:08:48.820
We use this tool after profiling confirms

199
00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:50.019
that nanite is the source

200
00:08:50.019 --> 00:08:51.580
of your performance bottlenecks,

201
00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:55.259
and it's designed to help show where overdraw is occurring

202
00:08:55.259 --> 00:08:58.580
and how it's impacting nanite's efficiency.

203
00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:00.779
What it does is it highlights areas

204
00:09:00.779 --> 00:09:03.940
where nanite is doing extra rasterization work.

205
00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:06.179
Basically that means areas where multiple layers

206
00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:09.460
of geometry overlap or where there are small triangles

207
00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:10.899
that are densely packed.

208
00:09:10.899 --> 00:09:15.059
Now, some amount of overdraw is normal and expected,

209
00:09:15.059 --> 00:09:17.179
especially in complex scenes.

210
00:09:17.179 --> 00:09:19.899
However, excessive overdraw leads

211
00:09:19.899 --> 00:09:23.059
to higher nanite culling and rasterization costs,

212
00:09:23.059 --> 00:09:25.279
as well as reduced effectiveness

213
00:09:25.279 --> 00:09:27.659
of nanite's independent scene scaling.

214
00:09:27.659 --> 00:09:29.460
Essentially, the more overdraw you have,

215
00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:34.399
the less efficient nanite can batch renders and clusters.

216
00:09:34.399 --> 00:09:38.179
To interpret this view, we use it to identify areas

217
00:09:38.179 --> 00:09:41.600
with dense or overlapping meshes,

218
00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:44.379
small geometry that might be unnecessarily detailed,

219
00:09:44.379 --> 00:09:46.919
and regions where simplifying assets

220
00:09:46.919 --> 00:09:49.360
or adjusting placement could reduce overdraw.

221
00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:52.840
Remember, blue indicates low overdraw, which is good,

222
00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:57.840
while red and orange show heavy or expensive areas.

223
00:09:59.190 --> 00:10:02.169
Remember that we wanna use this mode

224
00:10:02.210 --> 00:10:06.649
only after we have profiled to target performance issues,

225
00:10:06.649 --> 00:10:08.889
specifically tied to nanite,

226
00:10:08.889 --> 00:10:12.169
and that some overdraw is unavoidable,

227
00:10:12.169 --> 00:10:15.730
and minimizing it will help maintain efficient scaling

228
00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:18.370
and ensure your scene continues to render smoothly

229
00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:20.230
and cost efficiently.
